### What the defect actually was — measured, not inferred

A throwaway spec (deleted immediately; porcelain verified clean after) dumped
the three boxes at 1280×800 against the PRE-FIX dist:

  .token-prompt   x=0    y=0    w=220   h=256
  .sidebar        x=220  y=0    w=1060  h=256
  .shell__main    x=0    y=256  w=220   h=544

So the prompt consumed the sidebar's 220px column; the rail was displaced into
the 1fr column at 1060 wide; and `.shell__main` wrapped into the implicit
second row's FIRST column — the entire routed screen rendered in a 220px
strip. Terra's `02-inbox-desktop.png` shows precisely this.

**A note on the brief's prose.** It describes the consequence as the rail
"displaced out of its own column into the second one, with the main content
pushed past it". Measured, the main content was not pushed past — it was
squeezed to 220px wide in row 2. The brief understates its own defect. Nothing
turns on it (the fix and the assertion are right either way), recorded for the
record's accuracy.

## 3. The coverage-gap explanation — CONFIRMED IN SOURCE, and by measurement

The brief claims W-065 behaviour 15 measures the shell at three widths yet
never saw this, because `openSeats` dismisses the prompt first. Read at
apps/web/tests-serve/layout.spec.ts, inside the W-065 b15 describe block:

  async function openSeats(page) {
    await page.goto(`${served.baseURL}/#/seats`);
    await page.locator("#token-prompt-input").fill(served.token);
    await page.locator(".token-prompt__submit").click();
    await expect(page.locator(".token-prompt")).toBeHidden();
  }

All three of its cases (1280, 800, 500) call it before measuring. Confirmed.

I tested the obvious rival explanation — that b15's assertions were too weak
anyway, since the 1280 case asserts only `box?.width`, never `x`. **Refuted by
measurement:** pre-fix the sidebar's width is 1060, not 220, so
`expect(box?.width).toBeCloseTo(220, 0)` WOULD have failed. The dismissal in
`openSeats` is therefore the sole reason the defect survived, exactly as the
brief says. The explanation is complete and correct.

## 4. The red's stash mechanism — SOUND. Ruled under D-024/P-012's frame.

Superficially this has W-071's time order: the fix existed, then the red was
recorded. It is nevertheless a different class, and the difference is
principled rather than special pleading.

**D-024's forbidden act is defined by the recorded state never having existed
as a real product.** W-071's reds were taken against a hand-stubbed tree — an
implementation reverted to a fabricated no-op. Nobody ever shipped that tree,
so the artifact could not be re-derived and was verifiable only by the
builder's candour. That is what made it backfill.

**Here the recorded state IS the product.** Three independent checks:

  (a) the header's base is the trunk. `dirty:1b165057…`, and master's source
      tree computed in §1 is 1b165057… exactly. Not a branch commit — the
      shipped trunk.
  (b) the defect is the trunk's real behaviour. §2 reproduces it from
      `git show master:…`, never from their stash.
  (c) the failure is identical — same assertion, same line 119, Expected 0 /
      Received 220.

This is the **W-046-round-3 shape, which is already the accepted precedent**
(my own W-071 round-1 ruling, precedent (b): "accepted because the recorded
tree WAS the true pre-fix state"). It is not a new exception being carved; it
is the existing one applying on its own terms. D-024 waived an ORDER for
W-071's five reds specifically and its kill_when guards against a second
waiver of THAT class — nothing here asks for one, because nothing here is that
class.

**The honest limit, stated rather than glossed.** The artifact is verifiable by
EXECUTION, not by INSPECTION. `dirty:1b165057…` names the base but not the
delta; I had to take the delta ("master plus the spec file") from the brief's
prose and then confirm it by reproducing. A censor who did not run the
reproduction would still be relying on candour. The brief says this itself and
names P-012 as the fix, which is correct and is the right way to hold it.

By my W-067 test — verifiability by design, not by luck — this passes on the
strength of (b): the proposition the red certifies can be re-derived by anyone
with the trunk, without asking the builder anything. A prescription cannot be
executed; this instruction can, and I executed it.

The `-g` claim is also true from the artifact: `01.log`'s `# command:` line is
`npx playwright test --config=playwright.serve.config.ts -g W-075 behaviour 1`
— the title selector, never argv, so it covers this behaviour alone. Body is
assertion-level (a `toBeCloseTo` failure, not a stack, not a module-load
error), exit 1, sella `builder-d`.

